# Shell: one command at a time

Run **one** shell command per tool call when possible.

Long chains of trivial commands (`cd && git status && git diff && …`) often trigger
human approval even when each step alone would auto-approve. Prefer sequential
single commands; wait for the result before the next.

## Do

- One logical step per invocation (`git status`, then `git diff`, then …).
- Parallel tool calls only for **independent** reads (e.g. two `Read`s, or
  `git status` + `Read` of an unrelated file).
- Batch only when atomicity matters (e.g. a single intentional `git commit`
  script the user asked for) or the user explicitly wants a combined command.

## Don’t

- Pack multi-step “status + diff + log + merge + push” into one shell string
  just to save turns.
- Use `&&`-chains for exploratory or approval-sensitive work when steps could
  run separately.
